{0 Making a game on CamlCast}

From an empty directory to a small game — {e The Undercroft}: a dark vault, a
brazier, torches that answer the eye, a gate raised by a winch, and a way out
under an evening sky — one feature at a time.

A game on this engine {e describes} its world: it says what the world should be
right now, every frame, from nothing, and the runtime works out what changed.
That is React's idea over a raycaster, and it means the two things a game
usually has to invent for itself — somewhere to keep state and a way to get it
onto the screen — are already here.

{b Every step of this guide is a complete program.} The page quotes only what
each step adds; the whole game as that step leaves it is
[examples/stepNN_<slug>.ml], compiled with the rest of this repository so it
cannot drift from the engine. From a checkout,

{@sh[
dune build
dune exec examples/step01_room.exe
]}

runs any step — the [dune build] first is what puts [assets/] where the later
steps can read it.

{1 Four things to know before the first line}

{2 There is no level file format}

A level is OCaml. There is no editor, no [.map] to parse, and nothing to load at
start-up. A room is a value, a world is a value, and a level that varies with
the time of day is a function.

{2 The engine holds no content}

Not one colour, pattern, picture or room. What it has instead are the types
those things are values of, so a game supplies its own and two games can share
an engine without sharing a look. Every picture in this guide is drawn by a
pure function, until the last step reads two from disk.

{2 One library}

{[
  open Camlcast
]}

That is the whole of it. What is in {!Camlcast} is what a description is made
of; what is not is the platform underneath — [Engine], [Renderer],
[Framebuffer], [World], [Player]. Those are reachable by adding [camlcast.core]
to your dune file and saying so, which makes reaching past the layer a decision
with a diff rather than something autocomplete finds for you. You will not need
it.

{2 Three ways of being wrong}

A mistake in a level — a wall of no length, a doorway wider than the wall it is
cut into — raises [Invalid_argument] where you wrote it. Those are bugs in your
program, not conditions it can be in.

Something the world outside would not do — SDL refusing a window, a missing
picture — comes back as [Error (`Msg _)]. Those are conditions.

And the third, which is what {!Camlcast.Check} is for: a level that builds
perfectly and is still wrong. A room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a wall, a
step in the floor at a doorway. Step 22.

{1 Step 0 — A project that depends on the engine}

The engine is not on opam yet, so pin it from git.

{@sh[
mkdir mygame && cd mygame
opam switch create . 5.5.0                # or use a switch you already have
eval $(opam env --switch=. --set-switch)  # so the pin lands in it, whatever
                                          # OPAMSWITCH was pointing at before
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git
]}

{b If the pin fails on macOS}, it is opam's depext check: Homebrew ships
[sdl2-compat] under the name [sdl2] and opam cannot see it. Install the system
libraries yourself and tell opam to stop looking:

{@sh[
brew install sdl2 sdl2_image
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git --no-depexts
]}

On Debian or Ubuntu, opam resolves [libsdl2-dev] and [libsdl2-image-dev]
through apt without help. On Windows, use the MSYS2 environment.

Three files. [dune-project]:

{[
(lang dune 3.21)
(name mygame)
]}

[bin/dune]:

{[
(executable
 (name main)
 (libraries camlcast))
]}

And [bin/main.ml], which is Step 1.

{1 Step 1 — One room}

{[
  open Camlcast

  let checker ~color ~u ~v =
    Color.level color (if ((u / 16) + (v / 16)) land 1 = 0 then 240 else 170)

  let stone =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:(Texture.generate (checker ~color:(Color.rgb 150 150 160)))

  let ground =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:(Texture.generate (checker ~color:(Color.rgb 116 110 98)))

  let height = 4.
  let flat = Plane.horizontal 0.

  let level =
    P.(
      world ~atmosphere:Atmosphere.default
        ~spawn:("vault", Vec.make (-4.5) 0.)
        [
          room ~name:"vault"
            ~floor:(floor ~plane:flat ~material:ground)
            ~ceiling:(roof ~plane:(Plane.above flat height) ~material:stone)
            [
              boundary ~height ~material:stone
                (corners
                   [
                     Vec.make (-6.) (-6.);
                     Vec.make 6. (-6.);
                     Vec.make 6. 6.;
                     Vec.make (-6.) 6.;
                   ]);
            ];
        ])

  let () =
    match Run.play ~title:"The Undercroft" level with
    | Ok _ending -> ()
    | Error (`Msg message) ->
        prerr_endline message;
        exit 1
]}

[dune exec bin/main.exe] and walk about with WASD and the mouse. Escape leaves.

{2 What each piece is}

{!Camlcast.P} holds the parts a world is made of, and is written [P.( ... )]
around a description rather than opened over the whole file. Its names are
short and ordinary — [wall], [room], [text] — so a local open puts them exactly
where a world is being written and nowhere else, and marks where that starts
and stops.

Distances are in {b cells}. One cell is one texture repeat, and the eye stands
half a cell up, so a twelve-cell vault under a four-cell ceiling reads as a
hall.

A pattern is a pure function from a texel coordinate to a colour, and
{!Camlcast.Texture.generate} turns one into a tiling square.
{!Camlcast.Material.make} is what a wall or a floor is dressed in.

{!Camlcast.P.boundary} is a run of wall through the corners you give it, shut
into a loop unless you say [~closed:false]. {b The order does not matter}: it
measures the loop and winds it so the room is on the inside. A boundary wound
the wrong way round draws a room black from within, it is never what anybody
wanted, and this is why you will not meet it.

{!Camlcast.P.val-corners} is every point as a plain corner, which is what a
boundary with nothing to say at any of its sides wants. The next step is what a
corner is for.

{!Camlcast.Run.play} opens a window, renders the description once a frame until
the player leaves, and says how it went. The [Error] case is SDL's — the world
outside declining — and the [Ok] carries a {!Camlcast.Run.ending} this game
does not read yet. Step 24 will.

The whole program: [examples/step01_room.ml].

{1 Step 2 — Every wall its own}

A boundary gives every side the material it was handed, and any corner may say
otherwise:

{[
  boundary ~height ~material:stone
    [ corner sw ~material:brick; corner se; corner ne; corner nw ];
]}

{b A corner describes the wall {e leaving} it}, so [corner sw ~material:brick]
is the wall from [sw] to [se] — the south wall, brick, and the other three fall
back to stone. Everything {!Camlcast.P.wall} takes goes on a corner — a
handler, decals, a height of its own — which is what lets a boundary be
decorated or interactive without giving up the winding.

Reach for {!Camlcast.P.wall} itself only for what stands on its own. The vault
gains a plinth, low enough to see over, drawn from both sides:

{[
  wall ~height:1.1 ~material:slab (Vec.make (-2.) (-1.6)) (Vec.make (-2.) 1.6);
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step02_masonry.ml].
[demo/masonry.ml] is one pattern function worn at several colours.

{1 Step 3 — Pillars}

{!Camlcast.P.polygon} hands back {e corners}, not walls — [sides] of them,
[radius] from [center] to a corner — so a pillar is a boundary like any other,
the winding is still not your problem, and any leg of it could carry a handler.

{[
  let pillar center =
    P.boundary ~height ~material:slab
      (P.polygon ~center ~radius:0.7 ~sides:6 ~rotation:0.)
]}

Four of them ring the middle of the vault:

{[
  pillar (Vec.make 3. 3.);
  pillar (Vec.make 3. (-3.));
  pillar (Vec.make (-3.) 3.);
  pillar (Vec.make (-3.) (-3.));
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step03_pillars.ml]. [demo/haze.ml]
is a colonnade of them.

{1 Step 4 — The air}

One {!Camlcast.Atmosphere} per world: how far you can see, what colour the
distance is, and where the light comes from. The undercroft stops borrowing
{!Camlcast.Atmosphere.default} and gets its gloom:

{[
  let air =
    Atmosphere.make ~haze:(Color.rgb 22 22 30) ~fog_distance:10.
      ~min_brightness:0.12 ~light:(Vec.make (-0.5) (-0.85)) ~ambient:0.3
      ~directional:0.7 ()
]}

[directional] is how much of a wall's shading depends on which way it faces and
[ambient] is how much does not, so a six-sided pillar under a high
[directional] reads as six different greys — walk round one and watch.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step04_air.ml]. [demo/haze.ml] is
built to show exactly this.

{1 Step 5 — Pictures in the room}

A texture tiles a surface; an {!Camlcast.Image} is a picture with edges and an
alpha, drawn by the same kind of pure function:

{[
  let mural =
    Image.make ~width:48 (fun ~u ~v ->
        let ring r w =
          Image.disc ~cx:24. ~cy:24. ~r ~u ~v
          && not (Image.disc ~cx:24. ~cy:24. ~r:(r -. w) ~u ~v)
        in
        if ring 20. 3. || ring 11. 3. || Image.disc ~cx:24. ~cy:24. ~r:3. ~u ~v
        then (Color.rgb 208 178 122, 255)
        else Image.clear)
]}

A {b decal} is a picture fixed flat on a wall, placed in that wall's own terms —
[along] from the end it starts at, [z] up from the floor — and it rides on the
corner that describes its wall:

{[
  corner sw ~material:brick
    ~decals:
      [ decal ~along:6. ~z:2. ~half_width:1.2 ~half_height:1.2 mural ];
]}

[~facing:Back] hangs one on the far face instead, and [~glow] lifts it clear of
the room's light.

A {b sprite} stands in the room and turns to face you. It is sized by its
height and takes its width from the picture; [~base] floats it clear of the
floor, and [~glow] is how much light it makes of its own, which is none — a
sprite without one is lit by the room's [ambient], so turning a game's light
down turns its sprites down with it. A cold brazier stands on the floor, and a
mote of something else floats near the pillars:

{[
  sprite ~size:0.9 ~image:brazier_cold (Vec.make 0. 0.);
  sprite ~base:1.4 ~glow:0.9 ~size:0.25 ~image:mote (Vec.make 1.5 2.);
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step05_dressing.ml].
[demo/gallery.ml] and [demo/floating.ml] are this step at leisure.

{1 Step 6 — A second room}

Two rooms are joined at a {b doorway} they both describe and a {b link} that
says the two are the same one. The vault's east wall opens onto a corridor:

{[
  boundary ~closed:false ~height ~material:stone
    [
      corner ne;
      corner nw;
      corner sw ~material:brick ~decals:[ (* the mural *) ];
      corner se;
    ];
  doorway ~name:"east" ~width:2. ~opening:2.6 ~height ~material:stone se ne;
]}

{b Three sides run as an open boundary and the fourth is cut.} That is the
shape of a boundary with a hole in it: an open run still winds itself, and its
last corner describes no wall, so it must carry nothing. A closed boundary of
all four corners {e and} a doorway along one of them would be a solid wall
standing behind an opening — a doorway you can see through and cannot walk
through.

The corridor is written in a frame of its own, because every room is:

{[
  let c_sw = Vec.make 0. (-2.)
  let c_se = Vec.make 8. (-2.)
  let c_ne = Vec.make 8. 2.
  let c_nw = Vec.make 0. 2.
]}

There is no world-wide compass; only the link places it. Which means a
neighbour's floor is {e carried} across the doorway rather than written out
again:

{[
  let corridor_floor =
    P.through
      ~from:(P.opening ~width:2. se ne)
      ~into:(P.opening ~width:2. c_sw c_nw)
      flat
]}

{!Camlcast.P.opening} is the two ends the doorway will cut, in each room's own
terms, and {!Camlcast.P.through} carries a plane from one frame to the other. A
second floor written by hand to look right is one that will drift; derived, it
cannot — here it comes back flat, and the day the vault's floor tilts, the
corridor's follows. {!Camlcast.Check} reports a step in the floor at a doorway
either way.

The corridor answers with a doorway of its own, and the link marries the two:

{[
  doorway ~name:"west" ~width:2. ~opening:2.6 ~height ~material:stone c_sw c_nw;
  (* ... *)
  link ("vault", "east") ("corridor", "west");
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step06_antechamber.ml].
[demo/portals.ml] joins one room to a hub twice; [demo/slopes.ml] carries an
inclined floor through seamlessly.

{1 Step 7 — Your first component}

Everything so far has been one value. A component is a function from props to a
description, and it is what lets a part of a world keep something of its own.

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    P.sprite ~size:0.7 ~base:0.9 ~image:torch_stub pos
]}

Use it wherever a part of a world goes — four times, at the four pillars:

{[
  torch ~key:"ne" (Vec.make 2.1 2.1);
  torch ~key:"se" (Vec.make 2.1 (-2.1));
  torch ~key:"nw" (Vec.make (-2.1) 2.1);
  torch ~key:"sw" (Vec.make (-2.1) (-2.1));
]}

{b Declare components at the top level.} This is a rule and not a style. A
component is identified by its function being physically the same closure,
because a function is the only thing a component is — so one built inside
another render is a fresh closure every frame, is never the same component as
last frame, and is torn down and rebuilt along with everything under it. State
will appear not to work. {!Camlcast.Element.declare} exists partly to make the
right thing the easy one — and its type happens to enforce the other rule that
comes with identity, that a render function is monomorphic in its props. Props
are one value; when there are several things to say, they are a record.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step07_component.ml].

{1 Step 8 — State}

{[
  let brazier =
    Element.declare ~name:"brazier" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state false in
    Events.use_pressed (Input.Key Key.space) (fun () -> set_lit true);
    P.sprite ~size:0.9
      ~glow:(if lit then 0.85 else 0.)
      ~image:(if lit then brazier_hot else brazier_cold)
      pos
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_state} gives back what this brazier is holding and a way to
replace it; {!Camlcast.Events.use_pressed} runs a handler on the frame a
control goes down — the tap, not the hold. Space strikes the brazier alight.

Two rules come with hooks, and both are short.

{b Call hooks unconditionally, in the same order, every render.} Not inside an
[if], not inside a loop whose length varies. Nothing names a hook, so its
position is its identity. Break the rule and you get
{!Camlcast.Hook.Hook_order_changed} raised where you broke it, rather than a
value that was never there.

{b A setter shows up next frame.} It writes its slot and marks the tree; it
does not re-enter the render that is running. At sixty frames a second that is
sixteen milliseconds, and in exchange no render can fail to settle.

{b Walking is written with no hook at all.} That is the bindings' job, and it
reaches the description as movement without a component asking. Step 23 is
where the bindings themselves become yours.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step08_state.ml].

{1 Step 9 — Being looked at}

{!Camlcast.P.wall}, {!Camlcast.P.sprite} and {!Camlcast.P.doorway} each take
[on_gaze] and [on_use], because those are the three things an eye can stop on.
The torches take both:

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state false in
    let eyed, set_eyed = Hook.use_state false in
    P.sprite ~size:0.7 ~base:0.9
      ~glow:(if lit then 0.9 else if eyed then 0.25 else 0.)
      ~image:(if lit then torch_flame else torch_stub)
      ~on_gaze:set_eyed
      ~on_use:(fun (spot : Aim.spot) ->
        if spot.distance <= reach then set_lit (not lit))
      pos
]}

[on_gaze] is an {e enter} and a {e leave} — [true] when the crosshair arrives
and [false] when it goes, and nothing in between. It is not a poll, which is
why a setter is exactly the right shape for it.

[on_use] fires when the player works the use control — [E], out of the box —
while looking at this torch, and is handed an {!Camlcast.Aim.spot}: how far
away, how many doorways through, and for a wall where on it. The crosshair
reaches as far as the picture does — through doorways, up to
{!Camlcast.Config.max_portal_depth} of them — so whether a thing can be
{e worked} from here is a rule the game writes itself, and [distance] is what
this one is written with:

{[
  let reach = 2.5
]}

{b Give a [key] to anything whose siblings can come and go.} The leave half of
[on_gaze] is the one thing in a frame that has to recognise something from the
last one, and an unkeyed child is recognised by its position among its
siblings — insert one ahead of it and the [false] lands on whatever stands
where it stood. A list written out in order is fine; a list built from
something that changes wants keys. The rule and the reason are in
{!Camlcast.P}, under "Being looked at".

A crosshair joins the game so there is something to aim with — one line of HUD,
which gets its own step:

{[
  hud [ crosshair () ];
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step09_gaze.ml].
[demo/targets.ml] refuses by [crossed] as well as [distance]; [demo/chalk.ml]
marks walls with the rest of the spot.

{1 Step 10 — Time}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} runs once a frame, with how long the last one
lasted. The brazier stops holding forever and starts burning down — and since
the {e world's} air should burn down with it, the fuel moves up, into a [game]
component above the world. State lives at the lowest place everything that
reads it can reach.

{[
  let game =
    Element.declare ~name:"game" @@ fun () ->
    let fuel, set_fuel = Hook.use_state 0. in
    Events.use_pressed (Input.Key Key.space) (fun () -> set_fuel fuse);
    Events.use_frame (fun ~dt ->
        if fuel > 0. then set_fuel (Float.max 0. (fuel -. dt)));
    P.(
      world
        ~atmosphere:(air ~fire:(fuel /. fuse))
        ~spawn:("vault", Vec.make (-4.5) 0.)
        [ (* the rooms, as before *) ])
]}

The atmosphere becomes a function of the fire, the brazier becomes a component
that is {e told} what it holds — props down, state up:

{[
  type brazier_props = { fuel : float; pos : Vec.t }

  let brazier =
    Element.declare ~name:"brazier" @@ fun { fuel; pos } ->
    P.sprite ~size:0.9
      ~glow:(if fuel > 0. then 0.85 else 0.)
      ~image:(if fuel > 0. then brazier_hot else brazier_cold)
      pos
]}

and [Run.play] is handed [(game ())] instead of a value.

Scale the work by [dt] — a frame's length is not a constant, and a game that
ignores it runs at the speed of the machine. The handler is also called once
with [dt = 0.] before the first frame is drawn, because the description has to
be rendered once to know where to spawn the player; work scaled by [dt] costs
nothing there, which is one more reason it is the only way worth writing.

Effects — and {!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} is one — run {e after} the scene is
assembled and never during a render, because a render is meant to be a pure
function of props and state. A handler that set state in the middle of one
would make what a frame shows depend on the order its components happened to be
walked in.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step10_burning.ml].
[demo/phases.ml] is the same shape dressed differently.

{1 Step 11 — A box, for what merely accumulates}

The floating mote becomes a wisp, drifting a slow circle round the brazier:

{[
  let wisp =
    Element.declare ~name:"wisp" @@ fun (center : Vec.t) ->
    let phase = Hook.use_ref 0. in
    Events.use_frame (fun ~dt -> phase := !phase +. dt);
    let pos = Vec.add center (Vec.scale (Vec.of_angle (0.45 *. !phase)) 2.6) in
    P.sprite ~key:"wisp"
      ~base:(1.3 +. (0.25 *. sin !phase))
      ~glow:0.9 ~size:0.25 ~image:mote pos
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_ref} is a box made once and handed back unchanged every
render. Writing it asks for no frame — that is the whole difference from state,
and it is exactly right here {e because the loop renders every frame anyway}: a
phase that grows continuously would make every [set_state] a request for a
frame already coming. State is for what arrives in steps and must be shown; a
ref is for what merely accumulates while the world is being redrawn regardless.
A root that renders on demand — {!Camlcast.Mount}, step 22 — sees the
difference: nothing about a ref asks it to wake up.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step11_wisp.ml]. [demo/dust.ml]
moves a few hundred of these a frame.

{1 Step 12 — Drawing over the world}

{[
  let w, h = Events.use_viewport () in
  let dt = Events.use_dt () in
  let shown = Hook.use_ref 0. in
  shown := !shown +. (((fuel /. fuse) -. !shown) *. Float.min 1. (6. *. dt));
]}

and, at the end of the world's children:

{[
  hud
    [
      rect ~alpha:140
        ~x:(((w - 120) / 2) - 4)
        ~y:(h - 24) ~w:128 ~h:16 ~color:(Color.rgb 10 10 14) ();
      bar
        ~x:((w - 120) / 2)
        ~y:(h - 20) ~w:120 ~h:8 ~fraction:!shown
        ~color:(Color.rgb 230 170 80) ();
      highlight ();
      crosshair ();
    ];
]}

{!Camlcast.P.hud} is a child of [world], drawn over the finished frame in the
order it is written. {!Camlcast.P.rect} and {!Camlcast.P.bar} are what they
say; {!Camlcast.P.highlight} rings whatever the crosshair is on — a rectangle
round a sprite, and for a picture on a wall the trapezoid its corners project
to, because a wall recedes.

Coordinates are the {b buffer's}, not the window's. The engine renders at
whatever whole-number fraction of the window keeps it under
{!Camlcast.Config.max_render_height} and stretches the result, so a
thousand-pixel window is commonly a five-hundred-pixel buffer.
{!Camlcast.Events.use_viewport} is how a component asks how big the frame
really is, and is what the meter's centring is written against.

{!Camlcast.Events.use_dt} is the same [dt] the frame handler gets, read during
the render — here easing the drawn fraction toward the true one at a rate no
faster than the frames actually come.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step12_hud.ml].
[demo/overlay.ml] is a page of everything the layer can draw.

{1 Step 13 — Words}

The engine holds no font, exactly as it holds no colours or pictures, so the
game brings one: an atlas picture and the grid it is laid out on.

{[
  let typeface =
    Result.map
      (fun atlas ->
        Font.make ~fallback:'\127' ~atlas ~width:6 ~height:10 ~first:32 ())
      (Image.of_asset "assets/font.png")

  let () =
    match typeface with
    | Error (`Msg reason) ->
        prerr_endline reason;
        exit 1
    | Ok font -> (
        match Run.play ~title:"The Undercroft" (game font) with
        (* ... *))
]}

This is the guide's first picture from disk: {!Camlcast.Image.of_asset} looks
for [assets/font.png] relative to the executable — run [dune build] once first,
which copies [assets/] beside the examples in [_build], or point
[CAMLCAST_ASSETS] at a directory that holds it. A missing file is the second
way of being wrong, so it comes back as [Error] and is dealt with before any
window opens. The font travels into [game] as props.

While the brazier is cold, the game explains itself, wrapped to the buffer:

{[
  let lines =
    Hook.use_memo ~deps:w (fun () -> Font.wrap font message ~width:(w - 24))
  in
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_memo} is [compute ()], recomputed only when [deps] changes
— wrapping walks every character, so it is worth doing once per width rather
than once per frame. The lines are drawn with {!Camlcast.P.text} when [fuel]
is spent, and simply not drawn otherwise; the hook itself runs every render,
unconditionally, because that is the rule. {!Camlcast.Font.measure} is the
other half of the arithmetic, and centres the prompt in the next step.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step13_words.ml].
[demo/text.ml] wraps, measures and clips a whole page; [demo/typeface.ml] is
the same atlas described once for everyone.

{1 Step 14 — Asking what the eye is on}

[on_gaze] is the thing itself being {e told}. {!Camlcast.Events.use_aim} is the
description {e asking} — what was the crosshair on when the last frame was
drawn, and where on it — for saying something about whatever is looked at
without the thing having to say so:

{[
  let prompt =
    match Events.use_aim () with
    | Some { Aim.where = Aim.On_sprite; distance; _ } when distance <= reach ->
        Some "press E"
    | _ -> None
  in
]}

An {!Camlcast.Aim.spot} names the kind and the geometry — [On_wall] with its
[along] and [z], [On_sprite], [On_doorway] — and never the identity, so the
prompt appears for any sprite within reach and each torch's own [on_use] still
decides what E does there. One frame late, like the viewport, and for the same
reason: a description is rendered before the player moves through the world it
describes.

The prompt is centred with {!Camlcast.Font.measure}:

{[
  (match prompt with
  | None -> Element.empty
  | Some line ->
      let tw, _ = Font.measure font line in
      text ~font ~x:((w - tw) / 2) ~y:(h - 36) line);
]}

[Element.empty] is how a description says {e nothing} — a component cannot
decline to return, so absence is a value too.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step14_prompt.ml].

{1 Step 15 — A door in the doorway}

Past the corridor lies a courtyard, and an iron grille says not yet. The grille
is a {!Camlcast.Texture.generate_masked} material — colour where the bars are,
alpha [0] between them, so the way out is visible long before it opens:

{[
  let grille =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:
        (Texture.generate_masked (fun ~u ~v ->
             if u mod 16 < 3 || v mod 32 < 3 then (Color.rgb 44 46 52, 255)
             else (Color.rgb 0 0 0, 0)))
]}

A {!Camlcast.Door} is a material and a state, [Open] or [Closed], and it hangs
in an opening. {!Camlcast.P.doorway} would cut that opening and build the jambs
in one piece; here the corridor's east end wants a {e brick} lintel over
{e stone} jambs, which is the one case {!Camlcast.P.threshold} exists for — the
opening alone, with the walls either side the game's own to build, by the same
arithmetic the doorway would have used:

{[
  let gate_a, gate_b = P.opening ~width:2. c_ne c_se
]}

{[
  wall ~height ~material:stone c_ne gate_a;
  wall ~height ~material:stone gate_b c_se;
  threshold ~name:"gate"
    ~door:(Door.make ~state:Closed grille)
    ~lintel:{ top = height; material = brick }
    ~height:2.6 gate_a gate_b;
]}

[~height] is how tall the opening is; the {!Camlcast.lintel}'s [top] is how far
the wall over it rises. Own the jambs and you owe them: an end of a threshold
meeting no wall shows floor and sky to the horizon through the gap, which
{!Camlcast.Check} reports and a doorway cannot produce.

The courtyard describes the same opening from its side — and {b the same door}.
A door hangs in one opening; both rooms describe that opening, so both say so,
and {!Camlcast.Check} objects if they disagree:

{[
  doorway ~name:"west"
    ~door:(Door.make ~state:Closed grille)
    ~width:2. ~opening:2.6 ~height ~material:stone y_sw y_nw;
]}

The courtyard's floor arrives the way the corridor's did, carried through with
{!Camlcast.P.through}, and a second link marries gate to courtyard.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step15_gate.ml]. [demo/doors.ml]
opens and shuts them from both sides; [demo/barred.ml] is a room of grilles;
[demo/glass.ml] is the translucent pass at leisure.

{1 Step 16 — Held controls}

The gate wants a winch, and the winch wants the two ways of reading input that
are not the tap.

{[
  let cupped = Events.use_down (Input.Key Key.lshift) in
  let actions = Events.use_actions () in
  let winding = at_winch && Input.down actions (Input.Key Key.e) in
  let wound = Input.held_for actions (Input.Key Key.e) in
]}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_down} is the hold — whether a control is down right now,
read during the render. Shift {e cups} the brazier's flame: the frame handler
stops draining fuel while it is held.

{!Camlcast.Events.use_actions} is everything else — the whole
{!Camlcast.Input.actions} record, asked what the two hooks do not cover:
{!Camlcast.Input.held_for} is how long E has been held, and the frame handler
opens the gate when it has been worked long enough at the winch:

{[
  Events.use_frame (fun ~dt ->
      if fuel > 0. && not cupped then set_fuel (Float.max 0. (fuel -. dt));
      if winding && wound >= winch_takes && not gate_open then
        set_gate_open true);
]}

The winch itself is a sprite whose gaze handler is the {e game's} state,
because what it opens is the game's gate — and both doors' state now follows
[gate_open]:

{[
  sprite ~key:"winch" ~size:0.8 ~base:0.8 ~image:wheel ~on_gaze:set_at_winch
    (Vec.make 7.2 1.3);
]}

Both hooks are named after {!Camlcast.Input.pressed} and
{!Camlcast.Input.val-down}, because they are those two questions asked of this
frame. A control rather than a key throughout, so a mouse button can do a
component's job as readily as a key — step 23 does exactly that.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step16_winch.ml].
[demo/controls.ml] prints what it is asked with [Key.name].

{1 Step 17 — Crossing a doorway}

{[
  Events.use_crossed (fun crossing ->
      set_last_crossing (Some crossing);
      if crossing.Events.to_room = "corridor" then set_visited true;
      (* ... *));
]}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_crossed} runs once for each doorway the last frame went
through, in the same place in a frame that {!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} runs.
A crossing names its rooms and doorways by the names the description gave them
— a world is rebuilt every frame, and an index is what assembling one happened
to produce. One frame late, like everything read back from the world, and
{b empty while a description is placing the camera}: putting the eye somewhere
is a jump, not a walk, so there is no path along which a doorway was crossed.
{!Camlcast.Events.use_crossings} is the same list, read rather than handled.

The corridor remembers its first visitor — motes hang in it from the first
crossing on. And the status line in the corner reads three parts of the frame
at once, which is what {!Camlcast.Events.use} — the whole
{!Camlcast.Events.type-t} record in one ask — is for:

{[
  let frame = Events.use () in
  let status =
    let ms = int_of_float (frame.Events.dt *. 1000.) in
    let bw, bh = frame.Events.viewport in
    (* ... *)
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step17_crossings.ml].
[demo/trail.ml] and [demo/endless.ml] are crossings put to work.

{1 Step 18 — Reaching outside}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_effect} is the seam where a component may reach outside the
description — a log, a subscription, a file — after the frame is committed,
never during a render. [deps] decide when it runs again, and the returned
cleanup is exactly as long-lived as what it cleans:

{[
  Hook.use_effect ~deps:gate_open (fun () ->
      if gate_open then prerr_endline "the gate grinds open";
      None);
]}

runs after the first frame and again each time [gate_open] changes. The wisp
takes the other shape — [~deps:()] is "once, on mount", and its cleanup runs
when the component leaves the tree:

{[
  Hook.use_effect ~deps:() (fun () ->
      prerr_endline "a wisp drifts in";
      Some (fun () -> prerr_endline "the wisp departs"));
]}

It leaves the tree because the open gate frees it:

{[
  (if gate_open then Element.empty else wisp (Vec.make 0. 0.));
]}

Raise the gate and stderr says goodbye — a subscription taken in the effect and
dropped in the cleanup would have lived exactly that long, which is the next
step's whole trick. To run an effect every frame instead, pass
[~equal:(fun _ _ -> false)]; the default is structural equality, which raises
on deps that hold a closure, so those need an [equal] of their own.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step18_effect.ml].

{1 Step 19 — Shared state, by hand}

Component state belongs to a component. The embers now scattered through the
undercroft are worth counting, and the count belongs to nobody in particular —
threading it down as props is how a codebase acquires arguments that exist only
to be passed on. So the count lives outside the tree, in fifteen deliberately
bare lines:

{[
  let embers_taken = ref 0
  let listeners : (unit -> unit) list ref = ref []

  let subscribe listener =
    listeners := listener :: !listeners;
    fun () -> listeners := List.filter (fun l -> l != listener) !listeners

  let take_ember () =
    incr embers_taken;
    List.iter (fun listener -> listener ()) !listeners
]}

An ember keeps its takenness to itself and tells the world about the count:

{[
  let ember =
    Element.declare ~name:"ember" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    let taken, set_taken = Hook.use_state false in
    if taken then Element.empty
    else
      P.sprite ~size:0.2 ~base:0.05 ~glow:0.8 ~image:ember_img
        ~on_use:(fun (spot : Aim.spot) ->
          if spot.distance <= reach then begin
            set_taken true;
            take_ember ()
          end)
        pos
]}

And the tally is where the last hook comes in. {!Camlcast.Hook.use_invalidate}
is a function that tells this component's root it has work to do — the seam for
anything the runtime does not own. Subscribe in an effect, invalidate from the
callback, unsubscribe in the cleanup:

{[
  let tally =
    Element.declare ~name:"tally" @@ fun (font : Font.t) ->
    let invalidate = Hook.use_invalidate () in
    Hook.use_effect ~deps:() (fun () -> Some (subscribe invalidate));
    let w, _ = Events.use_viewport () in
    let line = Printf.sprintf "embers %d" !embers_taken in
    let tw, _ = Font.measure font line in
    P.text ~font ~color:(Color.rgb 236 150 90) ~x:(w - tw - 12) ~y:12 line
]}

Under {!Camlcast.Run.play} every frame renders anyway, and the tally would
seem to work without any of this. The invalidate is what makes it {e correct}
rather than lucky: a root that renders on demand — {!Camlcast.Mount}, three
steps from here — redraws this text only because something asked.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step19_embers.ml]. The plumbing is
the point of the step; keep reading before keeping it.

{1 Step 20 — The store}

{!Camlcast.Store} is step 19, packaged: the state, the one function allowed to
change it, and whoever is listening.

{[
  type progress = { embers : int }
  type action = Ember_taken

  let advance state Ember_taken = { embers = state.embers + 1 }
  let game_store = Store.create ~initial:{ embers = 0 } ~reducer:advance
]}

Made once, at the top level — a store made inside a render would be a new store
every frame, holding [initial] forever. The ember trades its hand-rolled notify
for {!Camlcast.Store.dispatch}, safe from any handler or effect and never from
inside a render:

{[
  Store.dispatch game_store Ember_taken
]}

and the tally trades subscribe-invalidate-cleanup for one hook:

{[
  let embers = Store.use_selector game_store (fun s -> s.embers) in
]}

{!Camlcast.Store.use_selector} arranges to be woken exactly when its answer
changes. Select the smallest thing that will do: a component reading
[fun s -> s.embers] is untouched by an action that moves anything else, while
one reading [fun s -> s] is woken by every action there is. There is no
provider and no dispatch hook, because OCaml needs neither — the store is a
module-level value and reaching it is spelling its name.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step20_store.ml].

{1 Step 21 — Context}

Some values are not state so much as {e surroundings}: a palette, a typeface,
how alive the flames are. Threading them as props is the same disease the store
cured, but these change with where you are in the tree, not with time — which
is what {!Camlcast.Context} is for.

{[
  let flicker = Context.make 1.
]}

The argument is the default, for a render with no provider in sight —
{!Camlcast.Check} gets that one. The game binds it above the world — cupping
the brazier now dims every flame at once:

{[
  let alive = if cupped then 0.35 else 1. in
  (* ... *)
  Element.provide flicker alive
  @@ [ P.(world (* ... *)) ]
]}

and any component under it reads it back, with no one in between naming it:

{[
  let alive = Hook.use_context flicker in
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_context} claims no slot and answers the same way every
render, so it is the one hook that is harmless inside a conditional — write it
unconditionally anyway; the rule is easier to keep than to keep track of
exceptions to. Nothing subscribes and nothing needs to: a description is
rebuilt every frame, so a changed value is simply what the next render sees.
{!Camlcast.Events.context} — how time and input reach the hooks — is itself one
of these, bound by {!Camlcast.Run.play} around your description every frame.

The same step brings {!Camlcast.Element.fragment}, for a helper that returns
several primitives where one is expected — the corridor's pair of motes is one
thing to the description now, and the key on the fragment is how the pair as a
whole keeps its place when the list around it changes:

{[
  let mote_pair ~key a b =
    Element.fragment ~key
      [
        P.sprite ~key:"a" ~base:1.2 ~glow:0.7 ~size:0.2 ~image:mote a;
        P.sprite ~key:"b" ~base:1.6 ~glow:0.7 ~size:0.2 ~image:mote b;
      ]
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step21_context.ml].

{1 Step 22 — Prove it without a window}

Everything a description is can be read without SDL anywhere near. The step-15
door disagreement was found exactly this way, and the game now proves itself
before it plays:

{[
  let prove font =
    let level = game font in
    (match Check.report level with
    | [] -> ()
    | found ->
        prerr_endline (Check.format found);
        exit 1);
    let built = Mount.build level in
    assert (not built.Scene.finished);
    let mount = Mount.create () in
    let frame dt =
      Element.provide Events.context { Events.still with dt } [ game font ]
    in
    ignore (Mount.render mount (frame 0.));
    ignore (Mount.render mount (frame 0.1));
    Mount.destroy mount;
    let scratch = Store.create ~initial:{ embers = 0 } ~reducer:advance in
    Store.dispatch scratch Ember_taken;
    assert ((Store.state scratch).embers = 1);
    print_endline "the undercroft holds: ok"
]}

{!Camlcast.Check.report} renders the description — components, effects and all,
giving back what they took — and reads the assembled world for what a compiler
cannot see: a room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a wall, two doorways joined
to the same third, a door only one side admits to. Where it can, a diagnostic
names the {e component} that wrote the offending part rather than the room the
engine found it in.

{!Camlcast.Mount} is the rest of the loop with the window left out:
{!Camlcast.Mount.build} for one look at the {!Camlcast.Scene} a description
assembles to, {!Camlcast.Mount.create} and {!Camlcast.Mount.render} to keep
state between renders. And a fake frame is nothing but
{!Camlcast.Events.context} bound by hand — [Events.still] with a [dt] — which
is the whole trick by which time passes in a test. The scratch store says the
last piece: a reducer is a plain function, so the shared state is as provable
as the geometry.

It needs no window, so its natural home in your own project is [dune runtest] —
one case per level, and your levels are covered for the cost of writing that
down. Here it runs at startup, and

{@sh[
dune exec examples/step22_check.exe -- --check
]}

runs only the proof.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step22_check.ml].

{1 Step 23 — Controls, and the map}

Everything the loop acts on by itself — walking, looking, leaving, the use
control and the map — is one record, {!Camlcast.Controls.t}, handed to
{!Camlcast.Run.play}:

{[
  let controls =
    let hold key weight =
      { Binding.source = Binding.Hold (Input.Key key); weight }
    in
    Controls.make
      ~bindings:
        (Binding.make
           ~forward:
             {
               Binding.speed = 3.6;
               terms =
                 [
                   hold Key.w 1.; hold Key.s (-1.); hold Key.i 1.;
                   hold Key.k (-1.);
                 ];
             }
           ~leave:[ Input.Key Key.escape ] ())
      ~use:[ Input.Key Key.e; Input.Button Input.Left ]
      ~map:[ Input.Key Key.f3; Input.Key Key.m ]
      ()
]}

A {!Camlcast.Binding} axis is a list of weighted sources summed into one number
a second is scaled by — walking gains a second pair of keys here, and the left
mouse button starts working what E works. {b Replacement, not merging}, at
every level: a given [~forward] is the whole of that axis, so W and S are
restated beside I and K; a given [~bindings] is the whole of the bindings, so
Escape is said again — {!Camlcast.Controls.default} was the layer saying it for
you.

Press {b F3} — or now {b M} — in the running game and the room is drawn from
above: every wall with a short tick pointing the way its normal faces, doorways
green when linked and red when they lead nowhere, the player's disc with its
collision ring, and a mark on anything {!Camlcast.Check} has something to say
about. The ticks are the fastest way to see a boundary wound wrong — a row of
them pointing out of the room — which is also why you have not needed the map
yet. [~map:[]] is a game that has stopped wanting one, and it costs that game
nothing: the walk over every wall that feeds the map only happens while the map
is up.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step23_controls.ml].
[demo/bindings.ml] and [demo/controls.ml] go further.

{1 Step 24 — The ending}

The courtyard opens to the sky. A ceiling is either a roof or the absence of
one, and absence names what shows instead:

{[
  let evening = Sky.make ~sun_azimuth:2.7 ~sun_height:0.06 ()
]}

{[
  ~ceiling:(open_sky evening)
]}

Stepping out is a crossing, and it starts a clock:

{[
  if crossing.Events.to_room = "courtyard" && freedom = None then
    set_freedom (Some 0.)
]}

While the clock runs, the description places the eye itself:

{[
  (match freedom with
  | None -> Element.empty
  | Some t ->
      camera ~room:"courtyard" ~pos:(Vec.make 5. 0.)
        ~angle:(2.6 +. (0.2 *. t))
        ~pitch:(Float.min 0.35 (0.12 *. t))
        ());
]}

While a {!Camlcast.P.camera} is in a description the controls do not move the
player at all — the description says where the eye is, every frame, and a walk
it did not ask for would fight it. Nor is the world {e worked} through this
eye: [on_gaze], [on_use] and the crossings all fall silent, so a pan across a
room does not drag an enter and a leave over everything it sweeps past. See
{!Camlcast.Run.aiming} for that rule stated once. Take the camera out again and
the player carries on from wherever it was last put.

Three seconds up, the description says the one thing it has not yet said:

{[
  (match freedom with Some t when t >= 3. -> finish | _ -> Element.empty);
]}

{!Camlcast.P.finish} is declared, not called — a component that has reached its
ending describes an ending, in the same place and the same way it says
everything else. The frame it appears in is drawn, and then the run stops. And
the [ending] that [Run.play] has been handing back all along finally gets read:

{[
  match Run.play ~title:"The Undercroft" ~controls (game font) with
  | Ok Returned ->
      Printf.printf "out of the dark, carrying %d embers\n"
        (Store.state game_store).embers
  | Ok Closed -> ()
  | Error (`Msg reason) ->
      prerr_endline reason;
      exit 1
]}

[Returned] is the run ending on its own terms — [finish], or the leave control.
[Closed] is the window going away. The difference is the next step's whole
subject.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step24_escape.ml].
[demo/daylight.ml] is two rooms under different skies.

{1 Step 25 — A launcher}

A window and a run are two lifetimes. {!Camlcast.Run.play} is
{!Camlcast.Run.with_window} and {!Camlcast.Run.on} rolled together, and taking
them apart is what a title screen is:

{[
  let () =
    (* ... *)
    Run.with_window ~title:"The Undercroft" (fun window ->
        let rec descend () =
          match Run.on window ~controls:title_controls (title_screen font) with
          | Ok Returned -> (
              match Run.on window ~controls (game font) with
              | Ok Returned ->
                  Printf.printf "out of the dark, carrying %d embers\n"
                    (Store.state game_store).embers;
                  descend ()
              | other -> other)
          | other -> other
        in
        descend ())
]}

Title and game take turns on one window; coming out of the dark comes back to
the title, and the picture changes rather than the window blinking. The store
outlives every run, so the embers accumulate across descents.

The title screen is a world like any other — a brick foyer, a torch, a camera
swaying where a player would stand — plus the one part of {!Camlcast.P} this
game has not needed: {!Camlcast.P.cursor} frees the mouse for a screen drawn
over the world, instead of capturing it for looking around. While it is up
nothing in the world is worked — the crosshair is not the player's — and what
still answers is {!Camlcast.Events.use_aim}, which is asked rather than told.
Enter describes [finish], and [finish] on a shared window is how a run hands
the window back:

{[
  Events.use_pressed (Input.Key Key.return) (fun () -> set_descending true);
  (* ... *)
  cursor;
  (if descending then finish else Element.empty);
]}

The title's controls list no way to leave, so Enter and the close button are
the only two doors out of it — controls are per-run, which is the other thing
this step buys.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step25_launcher.ml].
[demo/menu.ml] and [bin/demo.ml] are the same shape at full size.

{1 Step 26 — Shipping}

The game has drawn everything but the font itself. Real art arrives the same
way the font did, and everything read from disk gathers into one record and one
failure:

{[
  type art = { font : Font.t; tiles : Material.t; poster : Image.t }

  let load_art () =
    let ( let* ) = Result.bind in
    let* atlas = Image.of_asset "assets/font.png" in
    let* tiles = Texture.of_asset "assets/tiles.png" in
    let* poster = Image.of_asset "assets/poster.png" in
    Ok
      {
        font = Font.make ~fallback:'\127' ~atlas ~width:6 ~height:10 ~first:32 ();
        tiles = Material.make ~pattern:tiles;
        poster;
      }
]}

The south wall wears the tiles, the mural becomes the poster, and a missing
file names itself before any window opens — which is the shape a bundled game
wants.

{!Camlcast.Asset} is the search order: [Contents/Resources] for a bundle,
beside the binary, one directory above it, and [share/<exe>] — plus wherever
[CAMLCAST_ASSETS] points, which wins outright. So [dune build] serves a
checkout, and shipping is [dune build --profile release] plus putting the
binary, the SDL2 libraries and [assets/] in one directory —
[tools/bundle-*.sh] in this repository does exactly that, per platform.

The whole program, and the whole game: [examples/step26_shipping.ml].
[demo/loading.ml] is pictures from disk with nothing else in the way.

{1 Where to go next}

- {!Camlcast.P} — every part a world is made of, in one page.
- {!Camlcast.Hook} — the slot machinery, and the one rule it enforces.
- {!Camlcast.Events} — the frame record, and every way of asking it something.
- {{!page-"building-the-engine"} {b Building the engine from scratch}} — how
  the picture is drawn and how the layer over it works, both rebuilt by hand
  with the derivations written out.
- [demo/] — two dozen small worlds, one per feature, each short enough to read
  in a sitting.
